@@ -19,10 +19,10 @@ The library uses the following mapping from JSON values types to CBOR types acco
 | null            | `null`                                     | Null                              | 0xF6       |
 | boolean         | `true`                                     | True                              | 0xF5       |
 | boolean         | `false`                                    | False                             | 0xF4       |
-| number_integer  | -9223372036854775808..-2147483649          | Negative integer (8 bytes follow) | 0x3B       |
-| number_integer  | -2147483648..-32769                        | Negative integer (4 bytes follow) | 0x3A       |
-| number_integer  | -32768..-129                               | Negative integer (2 bytes follow) | 0x39       |
-| number_integer  | -128..-25                                  | Negative integer (1 byte follow)  | 0x38       |
+| number_integer  | -9223372036854775808..-4294967297          | Negative integer (8 bytes follow) | 0x3B       |
+| number_integer  | -4294967296..-65537                        | Negative integer (4 bytes follow) | 0x3A       |
+| number_integer  | -65536..-257                               | Negative integer (2 bytes follow) | 0x39       |
+| number_integer  | -256..-25                                  | Negative integer (1 byte follow)  | 0x38       |
 | number_integer  | -24..-1                                    | Negative integer                  | 0x20..0x37 |
 | number_integer  | 0..23                                      | Integer                           | 0x00..0x17 |
 | number_integer  | 24..255                                    | Unsigned integer (1 byte follow)  | 0x18       |
@@ -60,6 +60,14 @@ The library uses the following mapping from JSON values types to CBOR types acco
 Binary values with subtype are mapped to tagged values (0xD8..0xDB) depending on the subtype, followed by a byte string,
 see "binary" cells in the table above.
 
+!!! note "Negative integer ranges"
+
+    CBOR encodes a negative integer `n` as the unsigned argument `-1-n`, so the
+    head byte changes where that argument crosses 24, 256, 65536 and 2^32 - not
+    where `n` itself crosses a signed-type boundary. That is why the negative
+    ranges above are not the mirror image of the positive ones: `-200` is one
+    argument byte, the same as `-100`.
+
 !!! success "Complete mapping"
 
     The mapping is **complete** in the sense that any JSON value type can be converted to a CBOR value.
